      }
    ],
    "text_frames": [
      {
        "id": "notes",
        "position": [
          2,
          1
        ],
        "params": {
          "text": "**CT01 \u2014 A capacitor takes time to charge**  \n**What does a one-second time constant mean?** Run **5 s at 10 Hz**, then open Scope.  \nPhysical voltage at t=1 s: 0.63212 V. Forward Euler at h=0.1 s: 0.65132 V.  \nChange tau value 1 \u2192 2; rerun. Does charging get faster or slower? State time = Scope time minus h.  \n[Open CT01 student lab](qrc:/s7studio/help/education/labs/CT01-first-order/README.md) \u00b7 [MIT teaching materials](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-003-signals-and-systems-fall-2011/resources/mit6_003f11_lec04/) \u00b7 [S7 integrator rules](qrc:/s7studio/help/docs/INTEGRATORS.md)",
          "text_format": "markdown",
          "width": 1487,
          "height": 260,
          "font_size": 18
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
